                                                          },\n                                                                            \"right\": {\n                                                                              \"OR\": {\n                                                                                \"raw_text\": \"previous enrolment in this study\"\n                                                                                }\n                                                                              }\n                                                                            }\n4389; 2013) 3.10.2013. URL: http://www.who.int/medicines/areasugroup/en/). The WHO has established a Global Strategy and Plan of Action on Public Health, Innovation and Intellectual Property (GSPOA) to promote access to essential medicines, vaccines and diagnostics. The GSPOA emphasizes the need for countries to develop and implement policies and strategies to ensure access to essential medicines, vaccines and diagnostics, including through the use of flexibilities within existing international agreements on intellectual property rights. In 2013, the WHO launched a new initiative to promote the use of flexibilities within existing international agreements on intellectual property rights, including the TRIPS Agreement. The initiative, known as the WHO Global Strategy and Plan of Action on Public Health, Innovation and Intellectual Property (GSPOA), aims to promote access to essential medicines, vaccines and diagnostics. The GSPOA emphasizes the need for countries to develop and implement policies and strategies to ensure access to essential medicines, vaccines and diagnostics, including through the use of flexibilities within existing international agreements on intellectual property rights. The WHO has developed a number of tools and resources to support countries in implementing flexibilities within existing international agreements on intellectual property rig